      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which ITR form should professionals file?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Professionals in specified fields (CA, doctor, lawyer, architect, engineer, technical consultant) with receipts up to Rs 50 lakh (Rs 75 lakh if 95%+ digital) can file ITR-4 under Section 44ADA with 50% deemed profit. Non-specified freelancers (writers, designers, marketers) use ITR-4 under Section 44AD or ITR-3 with actual books. Professionals above the threshold or wanting actual expense deductions file ITR-3."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the receipts limit for Section 44ADA?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Rs 50 lakh gross receipts for specified professionals. This extends to Rs 75 lakh if 95% or more of receipts are through banking/digital channels (UPI, bank transfer, card). For Hinjewadi IT consultants receiving all payments via bank transfer, the Rs 75 lakh limit applies. For doctors with mixed cash and digital, the Rs 50 lakh limit may apply."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can freelancers claim expenses under 44ADA?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Under 44ADA, the 50% deemed profit includes all expenses. You cannot separately deduct rent, staff salary, equipment, internet or any other expense. If your actual expenses exceed 50% of receipts, filing ITR-3 with actual books will result in lower taxable income. Analyse both options before choosing your route."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do freelancers need to pay advance tax?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Professionals under 44ADA must pay the entire tax in a single instalment by 15 March. Regular filers pay quarterly. If expected tax liability (after TDS) exceeds Rs 10,000, advance tax is mandatory. Missing it triggers interest under Section 234B (1% per month on shortfall) and Section 234C (1% per month on deferment)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is tax audit mandatory for professionals in Pune?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Under regular filing (ITR-3), tax audit is mandatory if gross receipts exceed Rs 50 lakh. Under 44ADA, no audit required if declared profit is at least 50%. Declaring below 50% with income above exemption limit triggers mandatory audit. Non-compliance penalty: 0.5% of receipts or Rs 1,50,000 (Section 271B)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is TDS under Section 194J?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Section 194J requires clients to deduct TDS at 10% on professional fees exceeding Rs 30,000 per client per year. This TDS appears in Form 26AS/AIS. When filing ITR, claim it as tax already paid. If a client deducted but did not deposit (not in 26AS), you cannot claim until the client deposits. Reconcile 26AS with invoices before filing."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
