<!-- SECTION 15A: LEGAL FRAMEWORK -->
<section class="content-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">Legal and Compliance Framework</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                
                <p><strong>Governing Law:</strong> IT Act, 1961; Section 44ADA (presumptive for professionals); Section 44AA (books); Section 44AB (tax audit); Section 194J (TDS).</p><p><strong>Specified Professions (44AA(1)):</strong> Legal, medical, engineering, architecture, accountancy, technical consultancy, interior decoration, CBDT-notified.</p><p><strong>44ADA Threshold:</strong> Rs 50 lakh (Rs 75 lakh if 95%+ digital). 50% deemed profit. No books, no audit.</p><p><strong>44AD for Non-Specified:</strong> Turnover up to Rs 3 crore. 8% (6% digital) deemed profit.</p><p><strong>Tax Audit (44AB):</strong> Mandatory if receipts > Rs 50 lakh (regular). Form 3CB-3CD by 30 Sep. Penalty: 0.5% of receipts or Rs 1,50,000 (271B).</p><p><strong>TDS (194J):</strong> 10% on professional fees > Rs 30,000/client/year.</p><p><strong>5-Year Lock-In:</strong> Once in 44ADA, must continue 5 years. Exit triggers audit if income > exemption.</p><p><strong>New Regime Default:</strong> Form 10-IEA for old regime. Once-in-lifetime for professional income.</p><p><strong>Late Filing:</strong> Rs 5,000 (234F). Interest: 234A/B/C at 1%/month.</p><p><strong>Portal:</strong> <a href="https://www.incometax.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">incometax.gov.in</a> | Authority: CIT Pune.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ SECTION -->
<section id="faq-section" class="content-section" style="background-color: #ffffff;">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <div class="faq-expanded">
                <aside class="faq-expanded__aside">
                    <h2 class="faq-expanded__title">FAQs - ITR for Professionals and Freelancers in Pune</h2>
                    <p class="faq-expanded__lead">Find answers to the most common questions about professional and freelancer ITR filing in Pune.</p>
                    @include('partials.bigin-form', [
                        'variant'  => 'compact',
                        'service'  => 'Professional ITR in Pune',
                        'city'     => 'Pune',
                    ])
                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Which ITR form should professionals file?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Professionals in specified fields (CA, doctor, lawyer, architect, engineer, technical consultant) with receipts up to Rs 50 lakh (Rs 75 lakh if 95%+ digital) can file ITR-4 under Section 44ADA with 50% deemed profit. Non-specified freelancers (writers, designers, marketers) use ITR-4 under Section 44AD or ITR-3 with actual books. Professionals above the threshold or wanting actual expense deductions file ITR-3.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the receipts limit for Section 44ADA?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Rs 50 lakh gross receipts for specified professionals. This extends to Rs 75 lakh if 95% or more of receipts are through banking/digital channels (UPI, bank transfer, card). For Hinjewadi IT consultants receiving all payments via bank transfer, the Rs 75 lakh limit applies. For doctors with mixed cash and digital, the Rs 50 lakh limit may apply.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can freelancers claim expenses under 44ADA?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>No. Under 44ADA, the 50% deemed profit includes all expenses. You cannot separately deduct rent, staff salary, equipment, internet or any other expense. If your actual expenses exceed 50% of receipts, filing ITR-3 with actual books will result in lower taxable income. Analyse both options before choosing your route.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Do freelancers need to pay advance tax?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Professionals under 44ADA must pay the entire tax in a single instalment by 15 March. Regular filers pay quarterly. If expected tax liability (after TDS) exceeds Rs 10,000, advance tax is mandatory. Missing it triggers interest under Section 234B (1% per month on shortfall) and Section 234C (1% per month on deferment).</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is tax audit mandatory for professionals in Pune?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Under regular filing (ITR-3), tax audit is mandatory if gross receipts exceed Rs 50 lakh. Under 44ADA, no audit required if declared profit is at least 50%. Declaring below 50% with income above exemption limit triggers mandatory audit. Non-compliance penalty: 0.5% of receipts or Rs 1,50,000 (Section 271B).</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is TDS under Section 194J?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Section 194J requires clients to deduct TDS at 10% on professional fees exceeding Rs 30,000 per client per year. This TDS appears in Form 26AS/AIS. When filing ITR, claim it as tax already paid. If a client deducted but did not deposit (not in 26AS), you cannot claim until the client deposits. Reconcile 26AS with invoices before filing.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Professional ka ITR kaunsa form mein file kare?</strong> 44ADA ke under ITR-4 agar specified profession hai aur receipts Rs 75 lakh se kam (95%+ digital). Nahi toh ITR-3.</p><p><strong>44ADA mein kitna profit declare karna padta hai?</strong> Minimum 50% of gross receipts. Isse kam declare kiya toh tax audit lagega.</p><p><strong>Content writer ya designer 44ADA use kar sakta hai?</strong> Nahi. 44ADA sirf specified professions ke liye hai (CA, doctor, lawyer, engineer, architect, technical consultant). Content writer 44AD (business) ya ITR-3 use kare.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

